# Selecting DOM Elements from JavaScript

Last lesson we said the DOM is what the user actually sees. Now we reach into it from JavaScript and pull out an element so we can work with it.

The way in is the **`document`** object, and two of its methods:

- **`document.querySelector(selector)`** gives us the **first** element matching a CSS selector, or `null` if nothing matches.
- **`document.querySelectorAll(selector)`** gives us an array-like object containing **every** element that matches, or an empty one if nothing does.

Remembering the history of JavaScript, you will not be shocked that there are other ways to do this: `document.getElementById`, `getElementsByClassName`, `getElementsByTagName`, `document.body`, and more. `querySelector` and `querySelectorAll` are more versatile and more modern than all of them, so those are the two we will use. You will see the others in example code you find elsewhere.

## Selecting With CSS Selectors

The argument is a string, and it can be any CSS selector you could write in a stylesheet. Here is a page to practice on:

```html id=sampledom
<div id="main_panel">
  <p class="box">A paragraph in a box.</p>
  <a href="#" class="highlight">A highlighted link</a>
  <ul>
    <li class="highlight">Item 1</li>
    <li>Item 2</li>
    <li class="highlight">Item 3</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="highlight box">A highlighted box</div>
</div>
```

Run this and read each line against that markup:

```{javascript autorun}
console.log(document.querySelector('p.box').innerText);
console.log(document.querySelector('.highlight').innerText);
console.log(document.querySelectorAll('.highlight').length);
console.log(document.querySelectorAll('ul .highlight').length);
console.log(document.querySelectorAll('li.highlight').length);
console.log(document.querySelector('#main_panel') !== null);
```
```+id=sampledom
```

Taking them in turn:

- `p.box` is the first `p` element with class `box`. The dot means "class".
- `.highlight` is the first element with class `highlight`, whichever kind of element that is. Here it is the link, because the link comes first in the document. The other three highlighted elements are not selected, because this is `querySelector` rather than `querySelectorAll`.
- `querySelectorAll('.highlight')` finds all four: the link, two list items, and the div.
- `ul .highlight` narrows it to highlighted elements *inside* a `ul`, which is the two list items.
- `li.highlight` narrows it a different way: list items that have the class, which is the same two here.
- `#main_panel` selects by ID. The `#` means "id".

If nothing matches, you do not get an error. `querySelector` gives `null`, and `querySelectorAll` gives an empty list:

```{javascript autorun}
console.log(document.querySelector('.no_match'));
console.log(document.querySelectorAll('.no_match').length);
```
```+id=sampledom
```

## NodeLists

What `querySelectorAll` returns looks like an array and behaves like one for our purposes, but it is technically something slightly different called a **NodeList**: a list of DOM nodes. It has a `length`, you can index into it, and you can walk it with `for...of`.

```{javascript autorun}
const highlighted = document.querySelectorAll('.highlight');

console.log(highlighted.length);
console.log(highlighted[0].innerText);

for (const element of highlighted) {
  console.log(element.innerText);
}
```
```+id=sampledom
```

## Timing Matters

Here is the mistake that catches everyone once. JavaScript runs from top to bottom, and so does the browser's work of building the page. **A script cannot select an element that has not been created yet.**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <script>
      const p = document.querySelector('p');
      console.log(p);          // null
    </script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

That logs `null`. When the script runs, the browser has not reached the `<p>` yet, so no such element exists in the DOM. A moment later the paragraph appears, but by then our line has already run and `p` is stuck holding `null`.

You can watch both moments happen. No HTML is attached to the cell below, so it starts with an empty page, exactly like the document at the instant the `<head>` script runs. The middle line stands in for the browser reading on and building the paragraph, and the last line runs at the moment a script at the end of the `<body>` would:

```{javascript autorun}
// The browser is at the <script> in the <head>. The body is still empty.
console.log(document.querySelector('p'));

// Reading on, the browser reaches the paragraph and creates it.
document.body.innerHTML = '<p>This is a paragraph.</p>';

// A script at the end of the <body> would run here.
console.log(document.querySelector('p'));
```

The selector is identical on both lines. The only thing that changed is what existed in the DOM at the moment each one ran. (`innerHTML` is how we add markup from JavaScript, which we get to in a couple of lessons. Here it is only standing in for the browser building that part of the page.)

There are several ways to fix this. The simplest is to move the script so it runs *after* the elements it needs:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>DOM Selection Example</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
    <script>
      const p = document.querySelector('p');
      console.log(p);          // the p element
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

Putting the `<script>` at the end of the `<body>` is a common habit for exactly this reason. If a selector is unexpectedly `null`, check the order before you check the selector.

Next we will look more closely at what we can do with the element objects these two methods hand back.

## Exercises

1. Select the paragraph by its ID and store it in `greeting`.

    ```html id=page1
    <p id="message">Hello from the DOM</p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    // Write your code here

    console.log(greeting.innerText);
    ```
    ```+id=page1
    ```
    ```+solution
    const greeting = document.querySelector('#message');

    console.log(greeting.innerText);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(greeting === null).should('equal', false).run('`greeting` should hold the paragraph element. Select it with `document.querySelector` and a `#` selector', 'Selected the element');
    wrap(greeting.innerText).should('equal', 'Hello from the DOM').run('`greeting` should be the paragraph with `id="message"`', 'Selected the right paragraph');
    ```

2. Select every list item that has the class `done` and store the result in `doneItems`. There are two of them.

    ```html id=page2
    <ul>
      <li class="done">Buy milk</li>
      <li>Walk the dog</li>
      <li class="done">Rake leaves</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="done">This paragraph is not a list item.</p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    // Write your code here

    console.log(doneItems.length);
    ```
    ```+id=page2
    ```
    ```+solution
    const doneItems = document.querySelectorAll('li.done');

    console.log(doneItems.length);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(doneItems.length).should('equal', 2).run('`doneItems` should contain exactly the two list items with class `done`. The paragraph also has that class, so your selector needs to mention `li` as well', 'Selected exactly the two list items');
    ```

3. A script logs `null` instead of the element it wanted. What is the simplest fix?

    ```{mcq}
    Opening this page logs `null` rather than the paragraph element:

    ~~~html
    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <html>
      <head>
        <script>
          const p = document.querySelector('p');
          console.log(p);
        </script>
      </head>
      <body>
        <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
      </body>
    </html>
    ~~~

    What is the simplest change that makes it log the paragraph instead?
    correct: Correct! The script ran before the browser had built the paragraph. Moving the whole `<script>` element to just after the `<p>` means the element already exists by the time the selector runs.
    wrong: Think about what exists in the DOM at the moment that script runs, and compare the two versions of the page in "Timing Matters".
    [x] Move the whole `<script>` element down, to just after the `<p>` inside the `<body>`
    [ ] Change the selector to `document.querySelectorAll('p')`, which never returns `null`
    [ ] Add `id="message"` to the `<p>` and select it with `document.querySelector('#message')`
    [ ] Write the selector as `document.querySelector("p")`, with double quotes
    ```

